Ladybird now guesses http for a typed host with no known public suffix,
and keeps https for a host that is itself a suffix. That’s the rule both
Chrome and Firefox use to decide what not to upgrade to https. Chrome’s
AutocompleteInput::ShouldUpgradeToHttps() never upgrades an IP address
or a host that net::IsHostnameNonUnique() reports, which is any host
without an ICANN registry-controlled suffix, but not a registry name
such as “host” itself. Firefox’s HTTPS-First, schemeless typed input
included, skips any host for which the function nsHTTPSOnlyUtils::
UnknownPublicSuffixException() holds: one with no suffix that its
HasKnownPublicSuffix() knows. In Chrome 158’s chrome://omnibox, typed
devbox:8080, nas.local, printer.home.arpa and box.internal:3000 all
resolve to http URLs before any upgrade, while a bare devbox, router.lan
and box.localdomain with no port are searches. Chrome treats the name
printer.home.arpa as unique, since home.arpa is on the public suffix
list, so it keeps the https guess here too. Safari’s URL guessing isn’t
open source, so WebKit wasn’t compared.
